# ESC addresses: 10 = RF, 11 = FL, 12 = RR, 13 = RL. LEFT/RIGHT is PROVEN; FRONT/REAR IS NOT -
#   spin one wheel before making any per-corner claim.
# esc_count is 6. QGC's ESC health indicator is permanently lit - read esc_online_flags over DDS
#   instead, and NEVER index esc[] by position, key on esc_address.
# "ESC 1/2" IN A PX4 MESSAGE NAMES A SIDE, NOT A WHEEL, and 3/4 can never appear. EC_FUNC1/3 = 101
#   = Motor1 = RIGHT (addr 10 + 12); FUNC2/4 = 102 = Motor2 = LEFT (addr 11 + 13). Correct for a
#   differential drive - do not "fix" it. PX4 CANNOT SAY WHICH WHEEL DIED, ONLY WHICH SIDE.
# A POWERED-OFF ESC IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A DEAD BUS (online_flags clear, timestamp == 0).
#   ASK WHETHER THEY ARE POWERED BEFORE DIAGNOSING CAN.

#--- THE BLOCKER: LOCALIZATION ---
# RTAB-Map relocalization accepts 0 OF 20 ATTEMPTS ON THE MAP'S OWN BAG. It fails at GEOMETRY, not
#   appearance, which makes it a PIPELINE FAULT rather than an environment problem.
#   NEVER lower Vis/MinInliers to make it pass. Do NOT re-map the house. The DB-param-mismatch lead
#   is DEAD. Disproven and not to be re-proposed: map coverage, lighting/exposure, the rover sitting
#   inside the 0.308 m depth minimum, camera-gyro rate-dependent scale, yaw_source: wheels.
# The 2D grid itself is FINE - do not "fix" MaxGroundHeight (keep 0.10) or NormalsSegmentation.
# GOOD ROOM MAP: ~/rover_data/maps/house_map_v4.db (from bag map_run_20260809_185011). Walls are
#   single and the scale is tape-verified. That bag is NOT disposable - it is the replay adjudicator.
# THERE IS NO VIO RUNNING. Position is wheel ERPM plus camera-gyro dead reckoning, and RTAB-Map
#   CONSUMES our TF odom. This is a CPU and plate-in-frame decision, NOT a capability gap:
#   rgbd_odometry and icp_odometry are installed and have run here. rgbd_odometry alone costs 79.6%
#   of a core stationary; measured 09/21 load was 2.68 of 4 cores with FPV streaming OFF (FPV costs
#   a further 139% of a core).
# PX4 NEVER KNOWS WHERE IT IS - BY CONFIG, NOT DESIGN. EKF2_EV_CTRL = 4 (bit 2, VELOCITY ONLY);
#   position fusion (bit 0) is OFF. Target is 9 (position + yaw). EKF2_* IS SHARED WITH THE DRONE,
#   so this is an operator decision. Depth-camera VIO into EKF2 is the standard PX4 GPS-denied
#   method - their own worked example uses a D455.
# FC HEADING IS UNUSABLE INDOORS - it is the EKF's FUSED YAW, not the gyro. Two untested suspects
#   remain, bmm350 (EKF2_MAG_TYPE) and GPS (EKF2_GPS_CTRL = 7, fully enabled WITH NO GPS FITTED).
#   Both are EKF2_*, so both are shared with the drone.
# YAW IS UNRESOLVED on the rover side too: CORR = 7.4, RO_YAW_RATE_I STAYS 0, and /odom yaw is
#   unusable at speed.

#--- PRODUCT AND ARCHITECTURE SETTLED (20-21/09) ---
# Full text: ros2_ws/docs/deployment_site_scope.md sections 6-8, and px4_companion_interface.md s9.
# THE PRODUCT IS CASE B - a mission executed on a PREVIOUSLY BUILT MAP, which makes relocalization
#   unavoidable. The venue is a bounded SITE (a yard or a manufacturing unit), NOT a house. GPS is
#   an AID, never the architecture. INDOOR IS FIRST.
#   Nothing in the M0-M4 / L0-L5 / R1-R7 ladders or the A1-A9 feature list was reordered or
#   renumbered - only the VENUE assumption was wrong.
# THE DEPTH CAMERA IS PRIMARY FOR LOCALIZATION and builds the local costmap that planning runs on.
#   That half already works - T3 proved avoid-and-rejoin on it.
# LiDAR IS NOT PRIMARY but is NOT EXCLUDED - RTAB-Map fuses laser and visual. THE CAMERA IS NOT
#   REPLACEABLE BY A LiDAR: a 2D scan plane at 200-300 mm passes OVER a person lying on the floor
#   (the ISO 3691-4 70 x 400 mm test piece), and cannot see overhangs, negative obstacles or
#   anything semantic. The question is only ever "do we ADD a LiDAR".
#   CATCH WORTH REMEMBERING: our /scan comes FROM THE DEPTH CAMERA via depthimage_to_laserscan, not
#   from a LiDAR. Feeding it to RTAB-Map as a laser input is the same data twice - no independent
#   geometric constraint. That option only pays off once a REAL LiDAR is fitted.
# PLANNING IS NOT LOCALIZATION, and VIO REPLACES ENCODERS, NOT GPS. What is missing here is the
#   ABSOLUTE half, so the thing to publish to PX4 is the MAP-RELATIVE pose from relocalization, not
#   raw VIO. THE ROUTE IS THE BRIDGE WE ALREADY RUN - no new node, topic or firmware:
#   rover_ekf_bridge/src/main.cpp already uses LocalPositionMeasurementInterface and fills only the
#   velocity fields; position_xy and position_xy_variance sit beside them unused.
#   A WELL-DEFINED VARIANCE IS MANDATORY (no NaN) or the interface throws. After a long unmatched
#   stretch, hand PX4 a BIGGER VARIANCE rather than a confident lie.
#   EKF2_EV_POS_X/Y/Z are all 0.0 - correct TODAY because velocity comes from wheel odometry at the
#   body frame, but THEY MUST BE SET FROM THE MEASURED MOUNT the moment a camera-derived pose is fed
#   (front_overhang 0.337, cam_z 0.305, both known from G0). An unmodelled lever arm turns yaw rate
#   into spurious lateral velocity. EKF2_EV_DELAY is 0.0 and UNTUNED.
# PX4's OWN WARNING, verbatim: fusing GPS and vision "is really difficult, because when they
#   disagree it will confuse the EKF. From testing it is more reliable to just use vision velocity."
#   So ARBITRATE, DO NOT BLEND - one source per area, or design the handover deliberately.
# SAFETY RATING IS A SEPARATE, CERTIFIED PURCHASE. ISO 3691-4 expects a safety-rated scanner
#   (IEC 61496 Type 3, typically PL d) for personnel detection. NEITHER the Gemini 336L NOR an
#   STL-19P qualifies - it is a THIRD device. Confirm applicability before site selection; it drives
#   cost and mounting more than anything else.
# NEXT ACTION IS A DIAGNOSIS, NOT A PURCHASE. Buy nothing until the 0/20 is understood: a new sensor
#   feeding a broken pipeline buys nothing, and the diagnosis decides which sensor is even right.

