#--- RULER TRAPS THAT COST REAL TIME THIS PERIOD ---
# systemctl "active" PROVES NOTHING. Twice, every unit read active while depth, /scan and /odom were
#   all at 0.0 Hz. MEASURE RATES, NEVER is-active.
# THE ros2 CLI IS ITSELF AN UNRELIABLE RULER. node list ghosts for minutes; topic echo and topic hz
#   can return nothing on a provably live topic. Prove a node dead with pgrep; measure with a direct
#   rclpy subscriber. When the CLI goes quiet, reach for MAVLink.
# IT IS /fmu/out/vehicle_status_v1, NOT /fmu/out/vehicle_status. The unversioned name yields SILENCE
#   rather than an error, and a None default then reads as "disarmed" while the wheels are spinning.
#   PX4 message versioning is in effect across the board.
# BOOT-CLOCK TRAP: check /proc/uptime BEFORE reading any duration off a timestamp. This box
#   restamps early boot and once forged a convincing "service up 11 hours" on a 4-minute-old unit.
#   A GAP in a timestamped log is not a fault either - grep timesyncd for a clock STEP first.
# NEVER READ A QUIET TOPIC AS EVIDENCE - prove a non-zero baseline first. The same applies to any
#   detector: prove the detector was live across the window.
# /scan DOES NOT TRACK BEYOND ~3 m - park about 2.5 m out. It also does not come back on its own
#   after a scan-killing test.
# IS THE BOX ALIVE? tail -2 /var/log/ext5v/ext5v.csv - stamped every 2 s, independent of tmux and
#   of Claude, so a fresh row means ALIVE. Use it before ever cutting power on a silent terminal.
# A silent terminal reads as a dead box from the operator's chair: anything that may run over 30 s
#   must say so up front or go to the background.

